.TH CJSON_BOOL_VAL 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
cjson_bool_val, cjson_int_val, cjson_double_val, cjson_str_val \- read a scalar value from a DOM node
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cjson.h>

bool        cjson_bool_val(cjson node);
long long   cjson_int_val(cjson node);
double      cjson_double_val(cjson node);
const char *cjson_str_val(cjson node);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Each function returns the value that node holds, and calls
ccol_fatal_err() when the type of the node does not match.

cjson_str_val() returns a pointer to the copy of the string inside the
node; the caller must not free that pointer.  The string is always
well-formed UTF-8, whatever built the tree: the parser refuses a
document whose strings are not UTF-8, and cjson_create_string(3) and
cjson_set(3) refuse a string from the caller that is not UTF-8.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cjson_type (3),
.BR cjson_create_string (3),
.BR cjson_parse (3)
